import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Two Rust photo apps on the Mac — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="The same comparison as last time, on the desktop. On the Mac, LightPhotos hands every decode to Apple's ImageIO while LightCraft does it all in its own Rust, and that changes which one is faster, and where."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-07">October 7, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Two Rust photo apps on the Mac</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          The same comparison as <a
          href="/blogs/two-rust-photo-apps-in-the-browser.html">last
          time</a>, on the desktop. On the Mac, LightPhotos hands every
          decode to Apple's ImageIO while
          <a href="https://github.com/storytold/lightcraft">LightCraft</a>
          does it all in its own Rust, and that changes which one is faster,
          and where.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 9 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          On the same twenty Sony RAW files, LightPhotos filled its grid in
          0.73&nbsp;s to LightCraft's 1.24&nbsp;s, and used 0.9&nbsp;GB of
          memory to its 1.7&nbsp;GB, from a 14&nbsp;MB app to its
          43&nbsp;MB. Stepping to the next photo
          went the other way. Both reacted in about 0.35&nbsp;s, but
          LightCraft was finished in 0.38&nbsp;s and LightPhotos took
          1.1&nbsp;s. LightCraft fully develops the photos on either side of
          the one you're looking at. LightPhotos only gets their embedded
          JPEGs ready, and asks ImageIO to develop the RAW data once you
          arrive.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>The next-photo result flipped.</strong> In the browser,
          LightPhotos finished the next photo in 0.53&nbsp;s and LightCraft
          in 1.88&nbsp;s. On the Mac, LightCraft finishes in 0.38&nbsp;s and
          LightPhotos in 1.1&nbsp;s, though both put something on screen in
          about 0.35&nbsp;s. A Retina
          window wants more pixels than the camera's JPEG has, so on the Mac
          LightPhotos develops the RAW data too, and it does that only once
          you arrive.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Two ways to step through a shoot.</strong> LightCraft
          develops the next and previous photos while you look at the current
          one, so each step is finished the moment you arrive. LightPhotos
          only does the work for the photo you're on. It shows something just
          as fast, and does less work you never see.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>ImageIO still pays for itself.</strong> It makes LightPhotos
          small, makes its thumbnails nearly free, and reads HEIC and Canon
          CR3 files, which LightCraft can't fully decode yet.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What changes on the desktop</h2>
      <p>
        In the browser, the interesting differences were about threads,
        workers and storage. On the desktop those mostly go away: both apps
        are ordinary Rust programs that use every core and draw with
        <code>wgpu</code> on Metal. What's left is the difference that matters
        most for a photo app, which is where the pixels come from.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>LightPhotos uses ImageIO for everything.</strong> On the
          Mac, every JPEG, HEIC and RAW file goes through Apple's ImageIO, the
          same library Preview and Photos use. The RAW library the other
          builds use isn't even compiled in. I wrote about what that one call
          does in <a href="/blogs/what-imageio-gives-you-free.html">What
          ImageIO gives you free</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>LightCraft brings its own.</strong> It runs the same
          decoders on the Mac as everywhere else: its own RAW decoder and
          demosaic, and Rust libraries for JPEG, PNG, TIFF, WebP and JPEG XL.
          A photo looks the same on every platform, and LightCraft doesn't
          depend on what Apple supports.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Both read your folders in place.</strong> LightPhotos opens
          a folder and keeps its thumbnail cache beside the photos.
          LightCraft adds them to a library catalog, without copying them
          unless you ask it to.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>LightCraft has far more finished features.</strong>
          LightPhotos is for going through a folder fast, keeping the good
          ones and giving them quick edits, and much of what a full editor
          does isn't there yet. LightCraft is already a full Lightroom-style
          editor: masks, tone curves, colour grading, healing, lens
          corrections, presets, versions and history, HDR and panorama
          merges, albums and stacks, and export to six formats. Keep that in
          mind with every number below.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Decoding</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Apple's ImageIO, for every format</td>
              <td>Its own, in Rust, for every format</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">A RAW at fit-to-window</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">The embedded JPEG, then ImageIO's develop at the window's size</td>
              <td>Its own develop, up to 2560&nbsp;px</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">The photos either side</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Embedded JPEG ready</td>
              <td>Fully developed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">HEIC</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Yes</td>
              <td>Not yet</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Canon CR3</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Yes</td>
              <td>Embedded preview only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Your photos</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">A folder, read in place</td>
              <td>A library catalog, files left in place</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">License</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">MIT or Apache-2.0, with no copyleft libraries in the Mac app</td>
              <td>MIT or Apache-2.0</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>How I measured</h2>
      <p>
        I built both from their current code with each project's own release
        script, the one that makes the download, and ran them on the same
        M4 Pro Mac with 48&nbsp;GB of memory, on macOS 26.3. The photos were
        the same twenty Sony A7 II RAW files as last time, copied to a new
        folder for every run so that no cache had seen them. Each app also
        got a fresh home folder, so it started with its default settings, and
        a window of 1600 by 1000 points.
      </p>
      <p>
        The method is the browser one, moved to the desktop. A script
        launches the app on the folder, double-clicks the first photo, then
        presses the right arrow eight times. It captures the window every
        150&nbsp;ms and calls a step done once nothing has changed for three
        seconds. This time it also records the first capture that changed, so
        you can see when the app reacted as well as when it finished. Each
        number is the median of five runs. The runs agreed to within a few
        percent, except one of LightCraft's opens, which took 0.86&nbsp;s.
        Every capture takes about 0.23&nbsp;s, so nothing measures
        below about 0.35&nbsp;s. The script is <code>tools/desktop-bench</code>,
        and it only looks at the window, so it works on any Mac app.
      </p>

      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Download (.dmg)</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">7.3&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>15.2&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">App, installed</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">14&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>43&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Launch to window</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.31&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.43&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Launch to finished grid, 20 RAWs</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.73&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>1.24&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Open the first photo: reacts / finished</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.42 / 1.12&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.48 / 0.51&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Next photo, each (median of 8): reacts / finished</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.35 / 1.11&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.37 / 0.38&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Memory afterwards</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.9&nbsp;GB</td>
              <td>1.7&nbsp;GB</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 248" role="img" aria-label="Time in seconds. Launch to finished grid: LightPhotos 0.73, LightCraft 1.24. Next photo, reacts: LightPhotos 0.35, LightCraft 0.37. Next photo, finished: LightPhotos 1.11, LightCraft 0.38.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">SECONDS, LOWER IS FASTER</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="46">Launch to finished grid</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="66">LightPhotos</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="120" y="58" width="234" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="362" y="67">0.73</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="86">LightCraft</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="120" y="78" width="400" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="528" y="87">1.24</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="122">Next photo, reacts</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="142">LightPhotos</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="120" y="134" width="113" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="241" y="143">0.35</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="162">LightCraft</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="120" y="154" width="119" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="247" y="163">0.37</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="198">Next photo, finished</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="218">LightPhotos</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="120" y="210" width="358" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="486" y="219">1.11</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="238">LightCraft</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="120" y="230" width="123" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="251" y="239">0.38</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        One caveat on the grid. LightCraft opens its grid scrolled to the
        end, so at this window size 11 of the 20 thumbnails were on screen,
        and its 1.24&nbsp;s is for those 11. LightPhotos showed all 20.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the next photo flipped</h2>
      <p>
        I turned on both apps' timing logs for this part. When you step to
        a photo, LightPhotos shows the JPEG the camera embedded in the RAW
        file within about 20&nbsp;ms. That's why both apps react at the same
        time: the first change you see is at the measurement floor either
        way.
      </p>
      <p>
        But the A7 II's embedded JPEG is only 1616&nbsp;px wide, and on a
        Retina screen this window's photo area is 3584&nbsp;px wide. So
        LightPhotos then asks ImageIO to develop the RAW data at that size,
        which took 0.5 to 0.6&nbsp;s per photo. It already has the embedded
        JPEGs of the photos on either side, but it doesn't start that develop
        until you arrive, so every step pays for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft develops the RAW data itself, at up to 2560&nbsp;px, which
        is about half as many pixels. Once a photo is on screen, it decodes
        and develops the next and previous ones in the background and keeps
        them ready. Pressing the arrow then costs only a GPU pass of about
        35&nbsp;ms, which is why its 0.38&nbsp;s is right at the floor. My
        script spends a few seconds on each photo, which gives it time to get
        ahead, and that's about the pace of someone looking at each one.
        Holding the arrow key down could outrun it. I didn't measure that.
      </p>
      <p>
        I don't think either is the wrong choice. Preparing both neighbours
        means at least one of the two develops is thrown away on every step,
        and when you hold the arrow key or jump around the grid, most of them
        are. That's CPU time and battery spent on photos you never look at.
        Meanwhile LightPhotos has something on screen in the same time as
        LightCraft, and the camera's JPEG is plenty to judge framing and
        expression, which is most of culling. The half second after that is
        the photo sharpening to full Retina detail while you're already
        looking at it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If LightPhotos did prepare its neighbours, it wouldn't cost what it
        costs LightCraft, since it keeps finished 8-bit images: about
        34&nbsp;MB each at this size, so roughly 70&nbsp;MB for two. Whether
        that's worth the wasted develops depends on how people move through a
        folder, and that's worth measuring before changing anything.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the grid and the memory go the other way</h2>
      <p>
        For a grid thumbnail, LightPhotos asks ImageIO for an image no bigger
        than the thumbnail, and ImageIO answers from the JPEG embedded in the
        RAW file. That took 12 to 22&nbsp;ms per file here, which is how
        twenty RAW files fill the grid in under a second. LightCraft also
        shows each file's embedded JPEG first, then replaces it with its own
        thumbnail developed from the RAW data. Its grid isn't finished until
        those land.
      </p>
      <p>
        Part of the memory gap is preparing ahead, and part is what each app
        keeps. LightCraft keeps the decoded RAW data for the current photo
        and both neighbours as 32-bit floating-point RGB, 12 bytes a pixel,
        about 52&nbsp;MB each at 2560&nbsp;px. That's what lets its sliders
        work on the full range of the sensor: highlights above white, and
        exposure changes without banding. On the Mac, LightPhotos gets
        finished 8-bit images from ImageIO, 4 bytes a pixel, and for the
        neighbours keeps only their small embedded JPEGs. On screen the two
        look the same. Those sources only account for about 150&nbsp;MB of
        the 0.8&nbsp;GB difference, though, and I didn't break down the
        rest.
      </p>
      <p>
        The app size is ImageIO again. LightCraft ships its own decoders for
        RAW, JPEG, JPEG XL and more, plus its own colour management, and its
        release build skips link-time optimisation and keeps some debug
        information. LightPhotos uses the
        decoders that already come with macOS, so they cost it nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>What ImageIO costs</h2>
      <p>
        ImageIO isn't free in every sense. The photos look the way Apple
        develops them, which isn't the same as LightPhotos on Linux, Windows
        or the browser. LightPhotos only supports the cameras Apple supports,
        when Apple supports them. And when ImageIO gets a file wrong, I can't
        fix it.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft gets the opposite trade. One decoder and one develop on
        every platform means the same photo looks the same everywhere, and a
        bug in it is one LightCraft can fix. The cost is a bigger app and the
        formats it hasn't written yet. HEIC needs an HEVC decoder, and there
        isn't a pure-Rust, permissively licensed one yet. CR3 shows only its
        embedded preview for now.
      </p>

      <h2>Wrapping up</h2>
      <p>
        On the Mac, LightPhotos leans on the operating system and LightCraft
        leans on itself, and the numbers follow. LightPhotos is smaller,
        starts faster, fills its grid faster and uses less memory, because
        Apple already did that work. Part of it is also that LightPhotos
        simply does less: a much smaller feature set fits in a smaller app.
        LightCraft is faster where it decided to do more work ahead of time,
        looks the same on every platform, and does far more once you start
        editing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stepping through photos shows the same split in miniature. LightCraft
        spends memory and work up front so every step is finished on
        arrival. LightPhotos does only what you ask for and shows the
        camera's JPEG while it catches up. Both feel fast, for different
        reasons.
      </p>

